course: dictionary
lesson: 11809
title: Biased
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Biased means unfair. A biased person, report or referee favours one side over another, often without a good reason. «The referee was biased towards the home team and gave them every close call.» The referee did not judge the game fairly, so biased means not fair.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. In «Sam's report was biased, because he wrote only about the good things», the words only about the good things tell you Sam left out the other side. A report that shows only one side is biased. Ask yourself: is this fair to everybody?
explain: In maths and science, biased means not random. «The coin was biased, so it landed on heads far more often than tails.» A fair coin should land on each side about equally often, so a biased coin tips the chances towards one result.
fact: Biased means unfairly favouring one person, side or idea over another.
fact: «Biased» can also mean not random, like a coin or dice that lands one way more often.
fact: Common phrases include biased against, biased towards, a biased opinion and a biased coin.
fact: «Biased» is also the past form of bias, so «The rule biased the test» means the rule made the test unfair.
fact: Spotting bias helps you read the news carefully: ask who wrote it and whether both sides are shown.
exercise: «The referee was biased towards the home team.» What does biased mean here? | Very tired and slow | Watching from the stands | Wearing a red shirt | *Unfairly favouring one side
exercise: A dice lands on six far more often than any other number. What kind of dice is it? | Red | Fair | *Biased | Round
exercise: Which sentence shows a biased report? | «The report had nine pages.» | «The report gave both sides of the story.» | *«The report only praised one team.» | «The report was printed on Monday.»
test: Ella says «biased» means the same as «fair». Why is she wrong? | Biased means very fast | Biased means the same as fair, so she is right | Biased means quiet | *Biased means unfairly favouring one side, so it is the opposite of fair
test: What does the phrase «biased against» show? | A fair choice between two teams | A coin that lands on heads | A friendly welcome for everyone | *An unfair dislike or prejudice against one side
exam: Which word describes a coin that lands on heads far more often than tails? | Shiny | *Biased | Fair | Round
